om 498 to 501, posthumously demoted to Marquess of Donghun.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cha"><span class="enc-name">Xiao Cha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao Cha (519–562) was a grandson of Emperor Wu of the Liang dynasty who, after quarrelling with his uncle Xiao Yi, allied with the Western Wei.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-cong"><span class="enc-name">Xiao Cong</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao Cong was the third and last emperor of the Western Liang, a client state at Jiangling on the middle Yangtze.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-daocheng"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-daocheng.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Xiao Daocheng</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao Daocheng was a general who became the power behind the Liu Song throne in southern China.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-defei"><span class="enc-name">Xiao Defei</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao Defei was the wife of Yelü Chun, the Liao prince proclaimed emperor at Yanjing in 1122, founding the Northern Liao.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-he"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/xiao-he.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Xiao He</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao He was the chancellor of Liu Bang, founder of the Han dynasty.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/xiao-kui"><span class="enc-name">Xiao Kui</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Xiao Kui (542–585) was the second emperor of the Western Liang, a small state around Jiangling that existed as a vassal of the Northern Z